+      <h2 id="play-h">Explore it here</h2>
+      <p class="play-lead">
+        Drag the slider and the figure builds itself: a side length on the left, the square
+        or cube on the right, and in the middle the notation that joins them. Everything is
+        keyboard-operable except rotating the cube, which needs a pointer.
+      </p>
+      <!-- PLAY:START -->
+<a class="skip-embed" href="#how-to-play">Skip past the interactive app</a>
+
+<div class="play-frame" data-app="squares-cubes">
+  <iframe class="play-embed"
+          src="apps/squares-cubes/index.html"
+          title="Squares &amp; Cubes Explorer — See what the exponent is doing"
+          allow="fullscreen"
+          referrerpolicy="no-referrer"
+          loading="lazy"></iframe>
+</div>
+
+<p class="play-actions">
+  <a class="btn-play-full" href="apps/squares-cubes/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Open full screen</span><svg class="ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M14 4h6v6"/><path d="M20 4 11 13"/><path d="M18 14v5a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H5a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V7a1 1 0 0 1 1-1h5"/></svg><span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
+  <span class="play-note">Click or tab into the app first so it receives your keyboard. Works best on a screen at least 900&nbsp;px wide.</span>
+</p>
+<!-- PLAY:END -->
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="how-to-play" aria-labelledby="how-h">
+      <h2 id="how-h">How it works</h2>
+      <p class="section-lead">
+        The screen is one sentence read left to right: <em>this side length</em>, <em>squared</em>,
+        <em>gives this figure</em>. Change any part and the rest follows.
+      </p>
+
+      <div class="prose">
+        <h3>The three panels</h3>
+        <ul>
+          <li><strong>Left &mdash; the side length.</strong> A slider sets it (1&ndash;20 for squares,
+            1&ndash;10 for cubes) and lays that many units out in a line, so the number you are
+            about to square is itself a length you can count.</li>
+          <li><strong>Middle &mdash; the operation.</strong> An arrow pointing <span class="mono">&rarr;</span>
+            for squaring or cubing, <span class="mono">&larr;</span> for rooting, with the notation
+            underneath: <span class="mono">5&sup2;</span>, <span class="mono">5&sup3;</span>,
+            <span class="mono">&radic;25</span>, <span class="mono">&#8731;125</span>.</li>
+          <li><strong>Right &mdash; the figure and the count.</strong> The
+            <span class="mono">n&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;n</span> square or
+            <span class="mono">n&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;n&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;n</span> cube, with the total
+            spelled out as a product rather than just an answer.</li>
+        </ul>
+
+        <h3>Controls</h3>
+        <ul>
+          <li><strong>Squares / Cubes</strong> switches the shape &mdash; or press
+            <kbd>S</kbd> and <kbd>C</kbd>.</li>
+          <li><strong>Flip the arrow</strong> with the direction buttons or <kbd>D</kbd>. The
+            <em>Start with</em> and <em>Answer</em> labels swap sides with it, so which quantity
+            is the given one is never in doubt.</li>
+          <li><strong>Explode layers</strong> (<kbd>E</kbd>, cubes only) fans the cube into its
+            <span class="mono">n</span> cross-sections, each labelled
+            <span class="mono">n&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;n&nbsp;=&nbsp;n&sup2;</span>.</li>
+          <li><strong>Drag the cube</strong> to rotate it; <kbd>R</kbd> returns it to the opening
+            angle. Arrow keys move the slider once it has focus.</li>
+        </ul>
+
+        <div class="callout">
+          <p><strong>Watch the L.</strong> Raise the slider by one in Squares mode and the new
+          row, column and corner flash gold together. That L-shape is the
+          <span class="mono">2n&nbsp;+&nbsp;1</span> between <span class="mono">n&sup2;</span> and
+          <span class="mono">(n&nbsp;+&nbsp;1)&sup2;</span> &mdash; the reason consecutive squares are
+          spaced the way they are, shown rather than stated.</p>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="skills" aria-labelledby="skills-h">
+      <h2 id="skills-h">What it teaches</h2>
+      <p class="section-lead">
+        Exponents and roots are usually introduced as keys on a calculator. This puts the
+        geometry back underneath them.
+      </p>
+
+      <div class="prose">
+        <ul>
+          <li><strong>Why they are called squares and cubes.</strong> The names are not
+            decoration &mdash; <span class="mono">5&sup2;</span> is the area of a 5&times;5 square and
+            <span class="mono">5&sup3;</span> is the volume of a 5&times;5&times;5 cube. Most
+            students meet the notation years before they meet the reason for it.</li>
+          <li><strong>Roots as the question run backwards.</strong> Flipping the arrow does not
+            introduce a new operation; it asks the same relationship from the other end.
+            <span class="mono">&radic;144</span> is &ldquo;what side length gives this square&rdquo;.</li>
+          <li><strong>A cube is n layers of n&sup2;.</strong> The exploded view makes
+            <span class="mono">n&sup3;&nbsp;=&nbsp;n&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;n&sup2;</span> something you
+            count rather than something you are told.</li>
+          <li><strong>Squaring is not doubling.</strong> The single most common wrong answer.
+            Watching the figure grow from 6&sup2; to 7&sup2; &mdash; 13 new units, not 6 &mdash; is hard to
+            hold onto and still believe <span class="mono">7&sup2;&nbsp;=&nbsp;14</span>.</li>
+          <li><strong>The perfect squares and cubes worth knowing by sight.</strong> The full
+            range is on one slider, so 1, 4, 9, 16, 25 &hellip; is a thing you scrub through
+            rather than a list you memorise cold.</li>
+        </ul>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="tips" aria-labelledby="tips-h">
+      <h2 id="tips-h">Tips for getting the most out of it</h2>
+
+      <div class="prose">
+        <ul>
+          <li><strong>Predict before you drag.</strong> Say the total out loud, then move the
+            slider one step. Being wrong by a visible number of unit squares lands harder than
+            being wrong on paper.</li>
+          <li><strong>Step through the squares in order and watch the gaps.</strong>
+            1, 4, 9, 16, 25 &mdash; the differences are 3, 5, 7, 9. The gold L is that difference,
+            and once it is seen once it is hard to unsee.</li>
+          <li><strong>Use Explode before you use the cube.</strong> The assembled cube is
+            impressive but hides its own structure. Fanning it into layers first makes the
+            solid version readable afterwards.</li>
+          <li><strong>Start from the root direction sometimes.</strong> Leading with
+            <span class="mono">&radic;169</span> and asking for the side length is a different
+            question from asking for 13&sup2;, even though it is the same picture.</li>
+          <li><strong>Project it and let the class call the number.</strong> There is no score
+            and no wrong-answer state, so it works as a talking board rather than a task.</li>
+        </ul>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="limits" aria-labelledby="limits-h">
+      <h2 id="limits-h">Honest limits</h2>
+      <p class="section-lead">
+        What this app does not do. This section exists on every page here and it is not
+        marketing copy.
+      </p>
+
+      <div class="prose">
+        <h3>What it doesn&rsquo;t cover</h3>
+        <ul>
+          <li><strong>Perfect squares and cubes only.</strong> Every total on the slider is a
+            whole number squared or cubed. Roots that land between two perfect squares &mdash; the
+            case that makes <span class="mono">&radic;50</span> interesting &mdash; are not here yet.</li>
+          <li><strong>Nothing is asked of you.</strong> This is an explorer, not a practice set.
+            There is no question, no answer to enter, no score and no record of what you did.
+            It is built to be looked at and talked about.</li>
+          <li><strong>Squares stop at 20, cubes at 10.</strong> Past that the unit squares are
+            too small to count, which would defeat the point. Large-number exponents are a
+            different lesson.</li>
+          <li><strong>Whole numbers only.</strong> No fractional or negative bases, and no
+            exponents beyond 3 &mdash; the whole design rests on there being a shape to draw.</li>
+          <li><strong>No exponent rules.</strong> Multiplying powers, negative exponents and
+            scientific notation are the natural next topics and none of them are here.</li>
+        </ul>
+
+        <h3>Known rough edge</h3>
+        <p class="muted">
+          Below roughly 900&nbsp;px the layout drops to a single column and the three panels
+          stack, which works but loses the left-to-right reading that is most of the point.
+          Mobile is a deliberate fallback here, not a finished design &mdash; on a phone, landscape
+          is much better than portrait.
+        </p>
+
+        <!-- A11Y:START -->
+<h3>Accessibility, honestly</h3>
+<p>The page around this app meets <strong>WCAG 2.1 AA</strong>: a skip link as the first focusable element, a "skip past the interactive app" link before the frame so keyboard users are never trapped inside it, verified colour contrast on every token pair, visible focus rings, 44&nbsp;px touch targets, and full <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> support.</p>
+<p>The app itself is an existing project embedded here, and it does not meet that bar in full. Specifically: every control is a real button or a range input, the slider is fully operable with arrow keys, S/C/E/R/D drive the modes from the keyboard, there is no timer, and a live region announces the side length, the total and the notation on every change — so the arithmetic is available without seeing the figures. Two gaps. Rotating the cube is a drag with no keyboard equivalent, though it only changes the viewing angle and R returns it to the opening one. And the figures themselves — the square, the cube, the exploded layers — are decorative to a screen reader: the live region carries the count, but not the shape of it, which is the part the app exists to show.</p>
+<p class="muted">This app stores nothing — no local storage, no cookies, no analytics, no network requests of any kind.</p>
+<!-- A11Y:END -->
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+  </div>
+</main>
+
